    h4 An Analysis of Sina Weibo Censorship Using WeiboScope Search Data
    p
      img(src='/images/weibo1.jpg' width='100%')
    p
      | Starting at 4:28 PM May 19, 2012, I posted on 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com/u/2470768677') my Sina Weibo account
      |  two names as well as the Chinese words for &ldquo;Taiwanese independence.&rdquo; The first name I posted was &ldquo;Chen Guangcheng&rdquo; (in English and Chinese), the blind lawyer who escaped house arrest in Shandong province and made his way to the U.S. Embassy in Beijing. The second name was &ldquo;Bo Xilai&rdquo; (in English and Chinese), the former Party Secretary of Chongqing who recently fell from power. Less than 14 hours later, I received a message from 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo&rsquo;s
      |  system administrator informing me that my two posts on &ldquo;Chen Guangcheng&rdquo; were &ldquo;inappropriate&rdquo; and had been censored. While I can still see the two &ldquo;Chen Guangcheng&rdquo; posts on 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com/u/2470768677') my Sina Weibo account
      |  page, no one else can. Surprisingly, my posts on &ldquo;Bo Xilai&rdquo; and &ldquo;Taiwan independence&rdquo; were not censored.
    p
      | Herein lies the conundrum with censorship in China. We know that certain topics are censored from blogs hosted in China, Chinese search engines and Weibos. But we don&rsquo;t know where the line lies. Part of the reason is because the line is constantly moving. 
      a(href='http://www.baidu.com/') Baidu
      | , 
      a(href='http://www.sina.com.cn/') Sina
      |  and 
      a(href='http://www.tencent.com') Tencent
      |  could help identify the line by publishing a list of banned topics or keywords, but they don&rsquo;t. Rather, they 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com/1666067221/ykdfs4YSk') hire &ldquo;monitoring editors&rdquo;
      |  and rely on self-censorship to ensure that user generated content does not run afoul of Chinese authorities.
    p
      | Some computer scientists in academia have tried to make sense of censorship in 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
      |  by analyzing the data. In March 2012, 
      a(href='http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~dbamman/') David Bamman
      | , 
      a(href='http://brenocon.com/') Brendan O&rsquo;Connor
      |  and 
      a(href='http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~nasmith/') Noah Smith
      |  at 
      a(href='http://www.cmu.edu/') Carnegie Mellon University
      |  published a paper entitled 
      a(href='http://firstmonday.org/htbin/cgiwrap/bin/ojs/index.php/fm/article/view/3943/3169') &ldquo;Censorship and deletion practices in Chinese social media&rdquo;
      |  in 
      a(href='http://firstmonday.org/') First Monday
      |  after analyzing 56 million 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
      |  messages and found that more than 16% had been deleted. 
      a(href='http://jmsc.hku.hk/2010/02/bio-king-wa-fu/') King-Wa Fu
      |  and 
      a(href='http://jmsc.hku.hk/blogs/ricecooker/') Cedric Sam
      |  at the 
      a(href='http://jmsc.hku.hk/') University of Hong Kong&rsquo;s Journalism and Media Studies Centre
      |  have hacked the 
      a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
      |  that archives deleted posts on 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
      | .
    p(style='text-align: left')
      | For my MIT Media Lab final project, I&rsquo;ve tried to build on 
      a(href='http://jmsc.hku.hk/2010/02/bio-king-wa-fu/') King-Wa Fu
      |  and 
      a(href='http://jmsc.hku.hk/blogs/ricecooker/') Cedric Sam
      | &lsquo;s work by analyzing the data collected from the 
      a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
      |  to try to make some sense of 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
      |  censorship. Since its inception February 1, 2012 to May 20, 2012, the 
      a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
      |  has collected 12,032 deleted messages from 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
      | . The first thing I did was to simply plot all the deleted messages on a timeline from February 1, 2012 to May 20, 2012 and this is what I got:
    div
      img(src='/images/censored_weibo_timeline-1024x233.png' width='100%')
    div(style='text-align: right')
      a(href='http://public.tableausoftware.com/views/MASS61finalproject/censoredweibotimeline')
        span(style='font-size: x-small') Powered by Tableau
    p
      | My findings were consistent with the Carnegie Mellon team&rsquo;s findings. There are spikes in 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
      |  censorship as a result of media reports and rumors. During the Carnegie Mellon survey duration from June 27, 2011 to September 30, 2011, there was a rumor that former President Jiang Zemin passed away causing a spike in 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
      |  deletions. From February 1, 2012 to May 20, 2012, the following incidents in China caused in censors employed by 
      a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
      |  to work overtime:
    ul
      li
        strong February 6, 2012 &ndash; 
        | Chongqing Public Security Bureau head Wang Lijun goes to U.S. Consulate in Chengdu with information about the death of British businessman Neil Heywood that implicates Chongqing Party Secretary Bo Xilai.
      li
        strong March 8, 2012 &ndash; 
        | Chongqing Party Secretary Bo Xilai fails to show up at the National People&rsquo;s Congress, sparking rumors that he has fallen from power.
      li
        strong March 15, 2012 &ndash; 
        | Bo Xilai is removed from post as Chongqing Party Secretary.
      li
        strong March 18, 2012 &ndash; 
        | A Ferrari crashed on Beijing&rsquo;s Fourth Ring Road killing one and injuring two people.
      li
        strong April 22, 2012 &ndash; 
        | Blind lawyer Chen Guangcheng escapes from house arrest in Shandong province and makes his way to U.S. Embassy in Beijing.
      li
        strong May 14, 2012 &ndash; 
        | The Beijing Daily posts a message on its official Weibo charging that U.S. Ambassador to China Gary Locke is posing as an ordinary citizen and calls for Locke to disclose his wealth.
    div
      p
        | Interestingly, deletion of 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        |  messages tend to hit a low on Saturdays. I&rsquo;m not too sure why that is except that maybe censors want to take time off on weekends as well. If you want to maximize the length of time your message will remain on 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        | , probably the best time is to post the message after 11 PM Friday night.
      p
        | The second analysis I did with the 
        a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
        |  was to try and figure out how long it took the censors to delete messages on 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        | . Each 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        |  has a time stamp for when it was created. The 
        a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
        |  checks 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        | &lsquo;s timeline at most four times a day (but usually less due to limits that 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        |  imposes). Let&rsquo;s say for instance, a user posts a message on 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        |  at 8 AM. 
        a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
        |  checks 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        | &lsquo;s timeline at 9 AM, 3 PM, 9 PM, and 3 AM. If the message was deleted by the censor at 10 AM, it would show up on 
        a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
        | &lsquo;s &ldquo;deleted time&rdquo; as 3 PM.
      table(width='412' border='0' cellspacing='0' cellpadding='0')
        col(span='2' width='174')
        col(width='64')
        tbody
          tr
            td(height='17') shortest time
            td(align='left') 0:04:04
            td(align='right') hours
          tr
            td(height='17') longest time
            td(align='left') 3401:51:45
            td(align='right') hours
      p
      p
        | The fastest a post was deleted on 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        |  was just over 4 minutes. The longest time it took for the censor to get around deleting a message on 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        |  was over four months. For the posts created on May 20, 2012 and deleted on the same day, it took on average 11 hours for 
        a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
        |  to detect the deletion. It took the censors about 14 hours to delete my post &ldquo;chen guangcheng.&rdquo; Determining the average time it takes for censors to delete &ldquo;irresponsible&rdquo; messages is a bit tricky since we don&rsquo;t have data on exactly how long it takes for each post to be deleted. Out of curiosity, I pulled up three messages that took over four months to delete to see what they said:
