              | &ldquo;Hong Kong actor Stephen Chow said he supports Henry Tang&rsquo;s bid to be the next Chief Executive of Hong Kong. Chow admires Tang&rsquo;s way of doing things and open mindedness. Chow added that Tang is not stupid, but a smart person. Chow says that he wouldn&rsquo;t be friends with stupid people. Regarding whether Tang&rsquo;s infidelities will cause him to lose support, Chow says that it shouldn&rsquo;t matter because people are voting for the Chief Executive, not choosing a boyfriend.&rdquo;
    div
      p
      p
        | I&rsquo;m not too sure why it took so long to delete the posts. 
        a(href='http://jmsc.hku.hk/blogs/ricecooker/') Cedric Sam
        |  points out that the posts may have been in the 
        a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
        |  database to begin with and they just didn&rsquo;t turn up until several months later. The researchers at 
        a(href='http://jmsc.hku.hk/') University of Hong Kong&rsquo;s Journalism and Media Studies Centre
        |  are constantly adding new 
        a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
        |  to their list. Or, they could have just turned on the deletion marking system in the 
        a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
        |  so that it would have caught some censored posts that weren&rsquo;t caught before.
      p
        | To be sure, there is no way to tell for sure whether some of the posts were deleted by the users themselves instead of &ldquo;monitoring editors.&rdquo; Sina&rsquo;s API returns two types of error messages: &ldquo;Weibo does not exist&rdquo; and &ldquo;Permission denied.&rdquo; We assume that when a post is deleted by the user, the &ldquo;Weibo does not exist&rdquo; error message comes up. When a post is censored, the &ldquo;Permission denied&rdquo; error message comes up. 
        a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
        |  keeps track of all the deleted posts that have the &ldquo;Permission denied&rdquo; error message.
      p
        | If I had more time (and knew how to code), I would have liked to have analyzed more of the data that 
        a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
        |  came up with. Among the things I would have liked to explore are:
      ul
        li
          strong
            | Geographic distribution of deleted messages on 
            a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
            |  &ndash;
          |  The Carnegie Mellon paper also looked at geographic distribution of censored 
          a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
          |  and found that messages issued from Tibet, Qinghai and Ningxia are deleted at a higher rate. 
          a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
          |  also had data on the city and province that each message originated from. However, I didn&rsquo;t have enough time to figure out how to convert Sina&rsquo;s data in its city and province into a fungible type of data to transpose on a map.
        li
          strong
            | Relationships between the most censored 
            a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
            |  accounts &ndash;
          |  Using 
          a(href='http://research.jmsc.hku.hk/social/search.py/sinaweibo/#lastpermissiondenied') Weibo Scope Search
          | , we&rsquo;re able to rank the 3,524 users whose 
          a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
          |  messages are being deleted the most to last. One thing I&rsquo;d be interested in exploring is how many followers these 
          a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
          |  accounts have and whether they follow each other. It&rsquo;s not clear to me if the censors have compiled a list of influential 
          a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
          |  accounts and are tracking them daily or the censors are using key word searches to figure out what to censor.
        li
          strong
            | A deeper analysis into the most censored Chinese words on 
            a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
            |  &ndash;
          |  Several weeks ago, I did a word cloud of the most censored Chinese words on 
          a(href='http://www.weibo.com') Sina Weibo
          |  to see what came up. By far, the most censored words were the Chinese words for &ldquo;retweet&rdquo; followed by &ldquo;ha ha&rdquo; or some variation. It makes sense, but it&rsquo;s not very helpful. Given more time, I would have liked to dig a little deeper to see if there were any words or code words that consistently came up again and again after filtering out the &ldquo;retweets,&rdquo; &ldquo;ha ha,&rdquo; and other stop words.
      p
